<Fragment slot="heading">Blog ideas I wrote two years ago, and how they’ve <span style="font-style:italic">aged</span></Fragment>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">I found this page while flicking back through an old notebook. Seven blog titles, written a couple of years ago, and I never got round to any of them. Six of the seven have ‘generative AI’ in them, and the first promises to explain how it’s ‘revolutionizing’ product development in fintech. Spelt with a z, which tells you how many American slide decks I was reading at the time.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">What dated</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The technology aged faster than the questions. When I wrote the list, the exciting part was that a model could produce text, so every title is about generation. The interesting part now is that software can look something up and act on it, and a lot of the confusion about that shift lives in the vocabulary, which I’ve <a href="/think/why-agent-is-doing-too-much-work-as-a-word" class="src">written about before</a>.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The two titles on fraud and risk assumed AI would mostly be a tool for the defenders. Attackers got there quickly. In early 2024 an employee in engineering firm Arup’s Hong Kong office joined a video call in which the chief financial officer and several colleagues were all deepfakes, and made 15 transfers totalling about £20 million, as <a href="https://www.dezeen.com/2024/05/17/arup-victim-deepfake-video-scam/" class="src">Dezeen reported</a>. The newer problem is software acting for real customers. When Google <a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/announcing-agents-to-payments-ap2-protocol" class="src">announced its Agent Payments Protocol</a> in September 2025, it framed the challenge as proving that a user gave an agent authority for a particular purchase, and deciding who’s responsible when a transaction goes wrong. ‘Improving fraud detection with generative AI’ doesn’t come close to that question.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-family:Newsreader,serif;font-size:25px;line-height:1.25;max-width:52ch">"I was asking what AI could make. The questions that lasted were about what it should be allowed to do."</div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">What held up</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The chatbot one, mostly. ‘Chatbot is dead. Long live chatbot.’ was a throwaway line, and it turned out to be the only title with an argument inside it, so it’s the one I’m <a href="/think/the-chatbot-is-dead-long-live-the-chatbot" class="src">writing up first</a>. The ethics title has the right instinct and the wrong wording. ‘Balancing innovation and responsibility’ sounds wise and commits you to nothing. The version I’d write now asks who’s accountable when an agent gets it wrong, which is a question with actual answers. ‘Overcoming challenges’ is still true and still vague. I’d now say most of the difficulty sits in everything around the model rather than in the model itself.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">I’m going to work through the rest of the list, rewritten for where things are now, starting with that one.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">The nugget</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Dig out something you wrote a couple of years ago, a strategy deck or a list of bets, and sort it into what you’d still argue and what you’d rewrite. In my experience the ideas that last are framed as questions, and the ones that date tend to name a technology.</p>
</BlogPost>
